Z||000005||FieroSTETZ||09-29-2000||08:51 PM||||the 100 in "pc100" is the bus speed. bus speed is the rate at which data travels inside the computer. the bus speed it multiplied to get your final cpu speed, for example, a 500 mhz computer would be 100mhz X 5 = 500 mhz. the bus speed affects the percieved speed of the computer more than the actual clock rate. for example, i had a p200 that was 60mhz x 3 (= 180 actual) well, i was playing around, and benchmarked it, then set the computer to 83.3mhzX2= (approx 166) and re bechmarked it. the second benchmark, 166, was faster than the initial, 180. the reason for this was that the data internally was travelling at the higher frequency. i hope i got this all right, i have a hard time staying on track for more than a few seconds (extreme ADD) <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673"> ||||reg||1
